From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Bruno=20Greg=C3=B3rio?= <accounts@bruno.pw>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 14:19:16 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] regulator: dt-bindings: wl2868c: Describe the ET5907 second
 source
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Boards built around the WL2868C are also built with an ET5907 in its
place, at its own address and with the same seven LDOs and inputs. The
board cannot tell which it got, so let the node give both addresses and
leave finding the chip to the driver.

Signed-off-by: Bruno Gregório <accounts@bruno.pw>
---
 .../bindings/regulator/willsemi,wl2868c.yaml         | 12 ++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/regulator/willsemi,wl2868c.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/regulator/willsemi,wl2868c.yaml
index 8fcdd2196..40b868ad6 100644
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/regulator/willsemi,wl2868c.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/regulator/willsemi,wl2868c.yaml
@@ -15,12 +15,20 @@ description:
   and give 0.496 V to 1.512 V, LDO3 to LDO7 run from VIN34, VIN5, VIN6 and
   VIN7 and give 1.504 V to 3.544 V, all in 8 mV steps.
 
+  Some boards are built with an ET5907 in its place, at another address,
+  which has the same seven LDOs and inputs but programs them differently.
+
 properties:
   compatible:
     const: willsemi,wl2868c
 
   reg:
-    maxItems: 1
+    minItems: 1
+    items:
+      - description: The WL2868C's address.
+      - description:
+          The address of the ET5907 that may sit in its place, on a board
+          built with either.
 
   interrupts:
     maxItems: 1
@@ -75,7 +83,7 @@ examples:
 
         pmic@2f {
             compatible = "willsemi,wl2868c";
-            reg = <0x2f>;
+            reg = <0x2f>, <0x35>;
             reset-gpios = <&pm6450_gpios 3 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
 
             regulators {
